## Why This Is Asked

Assesss real-world ability to design resilient, low-tail-latency multi-region pipelines under stress while preserving tenant fairness and clear rollback paths.

## Key Concepts

- Backpressure and circuit breaking in streaming pipelines
- Per-tenant QoS and traffic shaping
- Exactly-once processing guarantees across Kafka and sinks
- Observability for SLO-driven tail latency
- Chaos testing and incident rollback discipline

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo: bounded per-tenant backpressure
function handleEvent(event, tenant) {
  const q = queues[tenant];
  if (q.length < MAX_PER_TENANT) {
    enqueue(event, tenant);
  } else {
    drop(event); // apply backpressure upstream
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you validate the degradation plan with synthetic workloads?
- What metrics would you use to decide when to restore normal traffic?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to triage, choose concrete signals, and perform automated rollback under pressure without customer impact.

## Key Concepts

- Per-tenant latency metrics (p95/p99)
- Backlog and ingestion lag
- Feature flags and weighted traffic routing
- Grace periods and rollback gates
- Postmortem and runbooks

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo-rollback snippet
function rollbackIfNeeded(metrics, thresholds) {
  if (metrics.p95 > thresholds.p95 || metrics.backlog > thresholds.backlog) {
    enableFeatureFlag('NEW_PATH', false);
    routeTraffic(OLD_PATH_WEIGHT = 100);
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you test this rollback plan in a staging environment?
- What telemetry would you collect for the postmortem?
